@@s001 Examples: flatMap and for in Scala
@@s002 <span>Week 9 · examples</span>
@@s003 Examples: flatMap and for in Scala
@@s004 The code of the <a href="../teoria/s09-monad.html">monad page</a>, with Option and List, which are monads already included in Scala. The function <code>sequence</code> and <code>replicateM</code> are the course exercises: they are not here.
@@s005 The whole file
@@s006 Run it with <code>scala-cli run Monad.scala</code>. The two functions <code>raddoppiaSePositivo</code> and <code>dimezza</code> return an Option: they are the steps of a chain that can fail.
@@s007 Monad.scala
@@s008 First step of the chain. If the number is positive it returns <code>Some</code> of the double, otherwise <code>None</code>: it is the container that can fail.
@@s009 Second step: it halves only the even numbers. <code>%</code> is the remainder of the division: <code>n % 2 == 0</code> holds when n is even.
@@s010 Chain of two <code>flatMap</code>s. 3 → Some(6) → Some(3). The value inside the Some passes from one step to the next without my having to discard the container.
@@s011 With −3 the first step gives <code>None</code>. <code>flatMap</code> on None is None: the second step does not run, and the result is None. The control of the flow is inside <code>flatMap</code>.
@@s012 A <code>flatMap</code> that always returns <code>Some</code> gives the same result as a <code>map</code>: that is why map can be written with flatMap and unit (<code>Some</code>). The comparison <code>==</code> answers <code>true</code>.
@@s013 The form <code>for … yield</code> is syntactic sugar: the compiler rewrites it into <code>flatMap</code> and <code>map</code>. <code>a &lt;- List(1, 2)</code> is a <code>flatMap</code> on the first list, <code>b &lt;- List(3, 4)</code> on the second.
@@s014 The same thing written by hand with <code>flatMap</code> and <code>map</code>: the result is equal to that of the <code>for</code>, so the comparison answers <code>true</code>.
@@s015 Right identity: passing each element to <code>List(a)</code> does not change the list. It is the law <code>x.flatMap(unit) == x</code>, with unit equal to <code>List(a)</code>.
@@s016 Left identity: <code>List(7).flatMap(raddoppiaLista)</code> gives the same list as <code>raddoppiaLista(7)</code>. It is the law <code>unit(y).flatMap(f) == f(y)</code> applied to an example.
@@s017 Support function: it returns a list with the double of n. It is a function <code>Int =&gt; List[Int]</code>, that is a step of a chain of Lists.
@@s018 The output
@@s019 Output of the run
@@s020 The chain with 3, with −3 and with 5: Some(3), None, Some(5).
@@s021 The <code>flatMap</code> with <code>Some</code> gives the same result as <code>map</code>.
@@s022 The <code>for</code> produces the four pairs, in reading order.
@@s023 The hand-written <code>flatMap</code> and the two identity laws: all <code>true</code>.
@@s024 To remember
@@s025 Sentence for the exam
@@s026 A monad is a functor with unit and flatMap: the result of the next step depends on the value of the previous one.
@@s027 Practical rule
@@s028 A <code>for</code> with several <code>&lt;-</code> is a chain of <code>flatMap</code>s. If you write it wrongly (for example with <code>map</code> where <code>flatMap</code> is needed), you get a nested container: <code>Option[Option[Int]]</code>.
